---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import GetSalvage from "../../components/GetSalvage.astro";
import first from "../../examples/clangd-first.txt?raw";
import refresh from "../../examples/clangd-refresh.txt?raw";
import refreshAdded from "../../examples/clangd-refresh-added.txt?raw";
import dotClangd from "../../examples/clangd-dotclangd.txt?raw";
import vscodeSettings from "../../examples/clangd-vscode.json.txt?raw";

const trimmed =
  "Real output on a 16 GB Apple M4 with Unreal Engine 5.8.4 and Epic's Third Person C++ template. The project path is shortened, and UnrealBuildTool's own log is trimmed where you see ...";
---
<Page
  title="Write Unreal C++ in VS Code or Neovim"
  compact
  description="How to use salvage clangd to set up clangd for an Unreal Engine C++ project on a Mac, so VS Code or Neovim can complete, navigate and check your code without Xcode's indexer."
  dek="clangd, the C++ language server, gives VS Code and Neovim completion, go-to-definition and live errors. salvage clangd sets it up for an Unreal project from the exact compile commands UnrealBuildTool uses, so you can write C++ without opening Xcode."
>
  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A Mac with Xcode installed.</strong> You don't open it, but UnrealBuildTool and clangd use its compiler
        and SDK. <a href="/charts/salvage-doctor/"><code>salvage doctor</code></a> checks your Xcode against your
        engine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An Unreal C++ project:</strong> a <code>.uproject</code> with at least
        one <code>Source/*.Target.cs</code>. A Blueprint-only project has no C++ to index; add a C++ class from the editor
        first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>clangd.</strong> Xcode includes one, at <code>/usr/bin/clangd</code> (Apple clangd 21 with Xcode 27.2).
        The one in Homebrew's <code>llvm</code> works too.
      </li>
      <li><strong>VS Code</strong> with the clangd extension, <strong>or Neovim</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>The <code>salvage</code> command.</strong> See <a href="#get-salvage">Get it</a> below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>1. Generate the compile database</h2>
    <p>In your project's folder, run <code>salvage clangd</code> with the editor you use:</p>
    <pre><code>cd ~/Projects/TP_ThirdPerson
salvage clangd --vscode     # or --neovim, or both</code></pre>
    <Terminal command="salvage clangd --vscode" output={first} caption={trimmed} />
    <p>
      Salvage runs UnrealBuildTool in its <code>GenerateClangDatabase</code> mode, which
      writes <code>compile_commands.json</code> into the project: every source file, with the exact flags it's compiled
      with. It indexes the editor target in the Development configuration unless you
      pass <code>--target</code> or <code>--configuration</code>. On this machine that took 7 seconds for 44 files. Pass <code>--dry-run</code> to
      see the UnrealBuildTool command without running anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your project has never been built, salvage warns that some <code>.generated.h</code> headers are missing.
      Build the target once so UnrealHeaderTool writes them. Until then, clangd flags every <code>UCLASS</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Open it in your editor</h2>
    <h3>VS Code</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Install the <strong>clangd</strong> extension by LLVM from the Extensions view.</li>
      <li>Open the project's folder: File, then Open Folder.</li>
      <li>Open any <code>.cpp</code> file. clangd starts and indexes the project in the background.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <code>--vscode</code> wrote <code>.vscode/settings.json</code>. It starts clangd with the flags below and turns
      off the Microsoft C/C++ extension's IntelliSense, so the two don't fight over the same files. Any settings you
      already had there are kept.
    </p>
    <Terminal command="cat .vscode/settings.json" output={vscodeSettings} marks={false} />

    <h3>Neovim</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Turn on project-local config in your own config: <code>vim.o.exrc = true</code>. That needs Neovim 0.9 or
        newer.
      </li>
      <li>Start Neovim in the project's folder. It asks whether to trust <code>.nvim.lua</code>; say yes.</li>
      <li>Open any <code>.cpp</code> file.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <code>--neovim</code> wrote <code>.nvim.lua</code>. It starts clangd with the same flags, through Neovim's
      built-in <code>vim.lsp.config</code> where it exists, or <code>nvim-lspconfig</code> if you have it installed.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Check it</h2>
    <p>clangd can check a file on its own, which is a quick way to see that the setup works before you open an editor:</p>
    <pre><code>clangd --check=Source/TP_ThirdPerson/TP_ThirdPersonCharacter.cpp</code></pre>
    <p>
      clangd prints a long log. The line that matters is the last one, which on this project
      read <code>All checks completed, 0 errors</code> after about 4 seconds with Xcode's clangd. Homebrew's clangd gave
      the same result, and so did the project's headers.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Keep it current</h2>
    <p>
      The database lists files and flags, so it goes stale when you add or remove a source file, or change
      a <code>.Build.cs</code>, <code>.Target.cs</code>, <code>.uproject</code> or <code>.uplugin</code>.
      Run <code>salvage clangd --refresh</code> after those. It only runs UnrealBuildTool when something that shapes
      the database has changed:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage clangd --vscode --refresh" output={refresh} caption="Nothing changed, so it skipped UnrealBuildTool. This run took 0.01 seconds." />
    <Terminal
      command="salvage clangd --vscode --refresh"
      output={refreshAdded}
      caption="After adding one .cpp file. UnrealBuildTool's log is trimmed at ..."
    />
    <p>
      Editing code inside existing files, or adding headers, doesn't need a refresh. clangd works those out on its
      own.
    </p>

    <h2>What it changes</h2>
    <p>Everything goes inside the project folder:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>compile_commands.json</code>, written by UnrealBuildTool.</li>
      <li><code>.clangd</code>, clangd's settings for this project.</li>
      <li><code>.vscode/settings.json</code>, with <code>--vscode</code>.</li>
      <li><code>.nvim.lua</code>, with <code>--neovim</code>.</li>
      <li><code>Intermediate/Salvage/clangd.json</code>, a record of what the database was made from, for <code>--refresh</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If <code>.clangd</code>, <code>.vscode/settings.json</code> or <code>.nvim.lua</code> already exists and would
      change, salvage backs it up first, next to the original, as <code>&lt;name&gt;.salvage-backup-</code> followed
      by a number. If nothing would change, it leaves the file alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>compile_commands.json</code> holds absolute paths to your engine and project, so it only makes sense on
      your machine. Keep it out of version control. <code>.clangd</code> is safe to share.
    </p>

    <h2>Undo it</h2>
    <p>There's no uninstall command, because everything is a file in your project. To take it all out:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Delete <code>compile_commands.json</code>, <code>.nvim.lua</code> and <code>Intermediate/Salvage/</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Delete <code>.clangd</code> and <code>.vscode/settings.json</code>, or, if you had your own before, put back
        the <code>.salvage-backup-</code> copy next to each.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The settings it writes</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>.clangd</code> keeps Unreal's warnings as warnings while you edit (Unreal builds with warnings as
      errors), turns off the include checks that Unreal's include conventions trip, skips the standard library index to
      save memory, and stays quiet about the headers UnrealHeaderTool generates. It's yours to edit.
    </p>
    <Terminal command="cat .clangd" output={dotClangd} marks={false} />
    <p>The editor settings start clangd with these flags:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--background-index</code>: index the whole project while you work.</li>
      <li><code>--header-insertion=never</code>: Unreal headers need their includes in a set order, so you add them.</li>
      <li><code>--completion-style=detailed</code>: show each overload separately.</li>
      <li><code>--pch-storage=disk</code>: keep precompiled preambles on disk instead of in memory, since Unreal's are large.</li>
      <li>
        <code>--query-driver</code> pointing at Xcode's clang (on a Mac): lets clangd ask the compiler in the database
        for its system include paths.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it's been tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        On macOS with Unreal Engine 5.8.4, on Epic's Blank and Third Person C++ templates. Both indexed with 0 clangd
        errors.
      </li>
      <li>
        On Windows and Linux, only in unit tests. It knows the right UnrealBuildTool script and platform for each, but
        nobody has run it there on a real project yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        Not yet on a large project. Generating the database and indexing will take longer there, and we don't have
        numbers for it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you try it on Windows, Linux or a big project, an <a href="https://github.com/Chapterists/salvage/issues">issue</a> with what happened would help.
    </p>

    <GetSalvage />
  </div>
</Page>
